---
// F5 and F7, the mission of Ch. 6: from a random start, after a while every node the same colour. F5: all 262 144
// rules, the 512 that are their own cousin (either colour may win), and the 27 whose average next density moves away
// from half and half, found without a simulation; then how sensitive the 27 are: the simple threshold rule is one of
// them and gets stuck, the winner is among the most sensitive. F7: the winner at work on a rewired grid, the threshold
// rule on the same start, and twenty fresh networks. Data: src/data/defence/consensus.json (scripts/defence/precompute.ts);
// the run and its verdict: src/scripts/defence/agree.ts.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import { texify, phi } from './tex';
---
<Slide id="ch-synchronisation" label="Can the rule predict the behaviour?" builds={4} class="f5" ch={6}>
  <div class="row grow f5-row">
    <canvas class="rules" aria-label="Every one of the 262 144 rules as one pixel: where it is born down, where it survives across"></canvas>
    <div class="col f5-side">
      <h2>Can the rule predict the behaviour?</h2>
      <p class="lede">The mission: after a while, all nodes the same colour.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li data-b="1"><b>262 144</b> rules, one pixel each</li>
        <li data-b="2"><b>512</b> are their own cousin: either colour can win</li>
        <li data-b="3"><b>27</b> move away from half and half, found <b>without a single simulation</b></li>
      </ol>
      <canvas class="sens" data-b="4" aria-label="The 27 from less to more sensitive: the simple threshold rule near the low end, the winner near the high end"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Can we really use what a rule looks like to predict what it will do? A test, in the form of a mission: from a random start, after a while every node should have the same colour, all on or all off. The obvious candidates are threshold rules: do what most of your neighbours do. On a network they get stuck, in patches that each agree with themselves but never with each other.</p>
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('Here are all 262 144 rules at resolution 9, one pixel each. First filter: we do not care which colour wins, only that both are equally likely, so the rule must treat on and off alike: it must be its own cousin. That leaves 512. Second filter, on the average next density: from half on and half off, the network must move away, towards all on or all off. That leaves 27, about one rule in ten thousand, found in a fraction of a second without a single simulation. Now we can afford to run every one of them.')} />
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('Running them shows what else matters, and the last picture puts the 27 in a row from less to more sensitive. The simple threshold rule is one of the 27: it even moves away from half and half the fastest of all, and still it gets stuck. The rules that work best are among the most sensitive: one neighbour more or less often changes what a node does, and that kicks it out of a local standoff. The best of them, $\\phi^9_{488,464}$, brought more than 96% of the rewired grids of the thesis (eight neighbours each) to agreement; most of the 27 do far worse, which is why we still simulate (Chapter 6, Physica D 2025).')} />
</Slide>

<Slide label="Watch it work" builds={3} class="f7" ch={6}>
  <div class="row grow f7-row">
    <div class="f7-box">
      <canvas class="grid" aria-label="The chosen rule on a rewired 30 by 30 grid"></canvas>
      <SimButton class="f7-btn" />
    </div>
    <div class="col f7-side">
      <h2>Watch it work</h2>
      <div class="f7-rules" role="group" aria-label="Rule">
        <button type="button" class="ctl" data-rule="winner" aria-pressed="true">The winner <span set:html={phi(9, 488, 464)} /></button>
        <button type="button" class="ctl" data-rule="threshold" aria-pressed="false">Simple threshold <span set:html={phi(9, 480, 496)} /></button>
      </div>
      <canvas class="trace" aria-label="Share of nodes on, round by round"></canvas>
      <div class="trials" data-b="2">
        <p><span data-won>0</span> of 20 fresh networks agreed</p>
        <div class="dots" data-dots>{Array.from({ length: 20 }, () => <i />)}<SimButton class="f7-btn2" /></div>
      </div>
      <p class="result" data-b="3"><b>Our result</b> 27 candidates out of 262 144, without a single simulation. The best one brings most networks to agreement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The winner at work, on a grid of 900 nodes in which a fifth of the links have been rewired (eight neighbours each), from a random start with half of the nodes on. Patches form and fight, and from round 31 every node is on. The curve on the right is the share of nodes that are on, round by round.</p>
  <p slot="say">The buttons switch to the simple threshold rule, on the same network from the same start: within a dozen rounds it freezes into patches, each content with its own majority, a few nodes blinking at the borders. Then twenty fresh networks, each with its own start, for whichever rule is chosen: the winner brings 19 of the 20 to agreement, the threshold rule none. These grids are a small illustration; the thesis ran 900 samples for every setting, on rewired rings, rewired grids and scale-free networks.</p>
</Slide>

<style>
  .f5-row { gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); align-items: center; }
  .rules { width: calc(860 * var(--u)); height: calc(900 * var(--u)); flex: none; }
  .f5-side { gap: calc(30 * var(--u)); flex: 1; }
  .steps { gap: calc(22 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(36 * var(--u)); }
  .sens { width: 100%; height: calc(190 * var(--u)); }

  .f7-row { gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); align-items: center; }
  .f7-box { position: relative; flex: none; }
  :global(.f7) .grid { width: calc(780 * var(--u)); height: calc(780 * var(--u)); }
  .f7-box :global(.f7-btn) { right: calc(-32 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(-24 * var(--u)); }
  .f7-side { gap: calc(24 * var(--u)); flex: 1; }
  .f7-rules { display: flex; gap: calc(14 * var(--u)); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .f7-rules .ctl { font-size: calc(30 * var(--u)); padding: calc(8 * var(--u)) calc(20 * var(--u)); }
  .f7-rules .ctl[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--sun); }
  .trace { width: 100%; height: calc(260 * var(--u)); }
  .trials p { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); }
  .dots { display: flex; gap: calc(10 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(10 * var(--u)); position: relative; align-items: center; }
  .dots :global(.f7-btn2) { position: relative; margin-left: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
  .dots i { width: calc(34 * var(--u)); height: calc(34 * var(--u)); border-radius: 50%; border: calc(3 * var(--u)) solid var(--line); background: var(--bg); }
  .dots i.ok { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .dots i.no { background: var(--panel); border-color: var(--muted); }
  .dots i.run { border-color: var(--accent); }
  :global(.f7) .result { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, buildOf } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { axes, curve, dot, label, line, px, py, type Plot } from '../../scripts/defence/plot';
  import { makeRng, lattice } from '../../scripts/net';
  import { type Rule, randomState, density, mirror } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { agreement, WINNER, THRESHOLD, type Agreement } from '../../scripts/defence/agree';
  import { Raster } from '../../scripts/raster';
  import { parse, mix } from '../../scripts/palette';
  import data from '../../data/defence/consensus.json';

  const cands = data.candidates as { B: number; S: number; bs8: number; slope: number }[];
  const is = (c: { B: number; S: number }, r: Rule) => c.B === r.B && c.S === r.S;

  // ── F5: the rule space, one pixel per rule; then the 512, the 27, and the 27 by how sensitive they are ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f5')) {
    const [c, sc] = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas')), ras = new Raster(512, 512);
    let at = 0;
    function paint() {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, side = Math.min(st.w - 60, st.h - 80), x0 = 60, y0 = 60;
      ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 2; ctx.strokeRect(x0 - 1, y0 - 1, side + 2, side + 2);
      label(st, 'where it survives →', x0, y0 - 30, { size: 28, color: t.muted });
      ctx.save(); ctx.translate(x0 - 22, y0); ctx.rotate(Math.PI / 2); label(st, 'where it is born →', 0, 0, { size: 28, color: t.muted }); ctx.restore();
      if (at < 1) return;
      const faint = mix(parse(t.panel), parse(t.line), at >= 2 ? 0.4 : 0.75);
      ras.fill(faint); ras.flush(); ras.blit(ctx, { x: x0, y: y0, w: side, h: side }, true);
      const k = side / 512, at2 = (B: number, S: number) => [x0 + (S + 0.5) * k, y0 + (B + 0.5) * k];
      // their own cousin: survive is the complement of born read backwards
      if (at >= 2) for (let B = 0; B < 512; B++) { const [x, y] = at2(B, 511 & ~mirror(B, 9)); dot(st, x, y, 3.2, t.accent); }
      if (at >= 3) for (const r of cands) { const [x, y] = at2(r.B, r.S); dot(st, x, y, 9, 'rgba(0,0,0,0)', t.danger); }
      if (at >= 4) { const [x, y] = at2(WINNER.B, WINNER.S); dot(st, x, y, 16, 'rgba(0,0,0,0)', t.ink); dot(st, x, y, 6, t.danger); }
    }
    // the 27 in a row, from less to more sensitive (Boolean sensitivity at eight neighbours), equal ones stacked
    function paintSens() {
      const st = stage(sc); if (!st) return;
      const { t } = st, x0 = 30, x1 = st.w - 40, y = 98, lo = 1, hi = 7.2, X = (v: number) => x0 + ((v - lo) / (hi - lo)) * (x1 - x0);
      line(st, x0, y, x1, y, t.muted, 2);
      st.ctx.fillStyle = t.muted; st.ctx.beginPath(); st.ctx.moveTo(x1 + 18, y); st.ctx.lineTo(x1, y - 9); st.ctx.lineTo(x1, y + 9); st.ctx.fill();
      label(st, 'more sensitive', x1 + 18, y + 48, { size: 28, color: t.muted, align: 'right' });
      const seen = new Map<number, number>();
      const named = (r: Rule) => is(r, WINNER) || is(r, THRESHOLD); // these two sit on the line
      for (const r of [...cands].sort((x, y) => +named(y) - +named(x))) {
        const key = Math.round(r.bs8 * 20), n = seen.get(key) ?? 0; seen.set(key, n + 1);
        const yy = y + (n % 2 ? 1 : -1) * Math.ceil(n / 2) * 15;
        const color = is(r, WINNER) ? t.danger : is(r, THRESHOLD) ? t.ink : t.line;
        dot(st, X(r.bs8), yy, is(r, WINNER) || is(r, THRESHOLD) ? 12 : 8, color);
      }
      const th = cands.find((r) => is(r, THRESHOLD))!, wi = cands.find((r) => is(r, WINNER))!;
      label(st, 'simple threshold: stuck', X(th.bs8), y + 52, { size: 30, weight: 600, align: 'center' });
      label(st, 'the winner', X(wi.bs8), y - 46, { size: 30, weight: 700, color: t.danger, align: 'center' });
    }
    slide(s, { set(b) { at = b; paint(); } });
    onSize(c, paint); onSize(sc, paintSens);
  }

  // ── F7: the winner (or the threshold rule) on a rewired grid; then twenty fresh networks ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f7')) {
    const [grid, trace] = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas'));
    const side = 30, N = side * side, p = 0.2, demo = data.demo;
    const net = lattice(side, 8, p, makeRng(demo.netSeed)), first = randomState(N, 0.5, makeRng(demo.startSeed));
    const RULES: Record<string, Rule> = { winner: WINNER, threshold: THRESHOLD };
    const buttons = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rule]'));
    let pick = 'winner', run: Agreement, rho: number[] = [], end: { ok: boolean; tick: number } | null = null;
    const reset = () => { run = agreement(net, RULES[pick], first); rho = [density(first)]; end = null; };
    function paintGrid() {
      const st = stage(grid); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, cell = st.w / side, a = run.state;
      ctx.fillStyle = t.panel; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, st.w, st.h); ctx.fillStyle = t.ink;
      for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) if (a[i]) ctx.fillRect((i % side) * cell, Math.floor(i / side) * cell, cell - 1.5, cell - 1.5);
    }
    function paintTrace() {
      const st = stage(trace); if (!st) return;
      const { t } = st, T = Math.max(60, rho.length), q: Plot = { x: 150, y: 10, w: st.w - 170, h: st.h - 100, x0: 0, x1: T, y0: 0, y1: 1 };
      axes(st, q, { xticks: [[0, '0'], [T, String(T)]], yticks: [[0, '0'], [1, '1']], xname: 'round', yname: 'share on' });
      curve(st, q, () => 0.5, t.line, 2, [6, 8]);
      st.ctx.strokeStyle = t.accent; st.ctx.lineWidth = 3; st.ctx.beginPath();
      rho.forEach((v, i) => { const X = px(q, i), Y = py(q, v); if (i) st.ctx.lineTo(X, Y); else st.ctx.moveTo(X, Y); });
      st.ctx.stroke();
      if (end) label(st, end.ok ? `all the same from round ${end.tick}` : `stuck from round ${end.tick}`, st.w - 10, 24, { size: 28, align: 'right', color: end.ok ? t.accent : t.danger, weight: 600, bg: true });
    }
    // three rounds a second; it stops when the verdict is in
    const ticks = ticker(s, 3, () => {
      if (end) return true;
      end = run.next(); rho.push(density(run.state));
      return !!end;
    }, () => { paintGrid(); paintTrace(); });
    // twenty trials of the chosen rule, a slice of rounds per frame so no frame waits
    const dots = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-dots] i')), won = s.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-won]')!;
    let job = 0;
    const clear = () => { cancelAnimationFrame(job); job = 0; dots.forEach((d) => d.className = ''); won.textContent = '0'; };
    function trials() {
      clear();
      const rule = RULES[pick], seeds = data.trials.winner;
      let i = 0, wins = 0, tr: Agreement;
      const begin = () => { const x = seeds[i]; tr = agreement(lattice(side, 8, p, makeRng(x.netSeed)), rule, randomState(N, 0.5, makeRng(x.startSeed))); dots[i].className = 'run'; };
      begin();
      const frame = () => {
        const t0 = performance.now();
        while (performance.now() - t0 < 4 && i < 20) {
          const v = tr.next();
          if (v) { dots[i].className = v.ok ? 'ok' : 'no'; if (v.ok) wins++; won.textContent = String(wins); i++; if (i < 20) begin(); }
        }
        if (i < 20) job = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
      };
      job = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
    }
    const mark = () => buttons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.rule === pick)));
    // another rule: back to the same start, and what ran runs on; once the trials are up, they run again for it
    buttons.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (b.dataset.rule === pick) return;
      pick = b.dataset.rule!; mark();
      const going = ticks.playing; ticks.play(false); reset(); paintGrid(); paintTrace();
      if (going) ticks.play(true);
      if (buildOf(s) >= 2) trials(); else clear();
    }));
    reset();
    const [gridBtn, trialBtn] = s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]');
    sim(gridBtn, 1, { reset() { ticks.play(false); reset(); paintGrid(); paintTrace(); }, play() { ticks.play(true); } });
    sim(trialBtn, 2, { reset: clear, play: trials });
    slide(s, { enter() { pick = 'winner'; mark(); reset(); clear(); }, leave() { ticks.play(false); cancelAnimationFrame(job); } });
    onSize(grid, paintGrid); onSize(trace, paintTrace);
  }
</script>
